reviewCommentsList.body:
Putting this back would, as suggested by the comment, cause problems in subsequent test cases, so I suspect this won't be possible.

If indeed not possible, then agreed that the test is pointless as-is, and:
```
// Before bob gets a trustline, have him try to cash a check.
// Should fail.
env(check::create(alice, bob, USD(20)));
env.close();
````
can be removed.

Overall, these tests are _fantastic_.
One thing I think could help with readability, would be to use a set of integers to keep track of what each account's owner count _should_ be. Then increment (or decrement) that value whenever a change is expected. This would allow the reader to look at each test case separately without needing to flip up to the previous test to check the prior value. Down the road, it would also allow reordering the test cases or inserting new test cases between the existing ones.
e.g.
```
        Account const gw1{"gw1"};
        std::size_t gw1Count = 0;
        Account const alice{"alice"};
        std::size_t aliceCount = 0;
        Account const bob{"bob"};
        std::size_t bobCount = 0;
[...]
            // gw1's check should be consumed.
            // Since gw1's check was consumed and the trust line was not
            // created by gw1, gw1's owner count should be 0.
            BEAST_EXPECT(ownerCount(env, gw1) == gw1Count);

            // alice's automatically created trust line bumps her owner count.
            BEAST_EXPECT(ownerCount(env, alice) == ++aliceCount);
```

One way to do the changes from line 200 (in the original code) to line 225 that requires fewer actual code changes is to just add `&& !ctx.view.rules().enabled(featureCheckCashMakesTrustLine)` to the condition on line 200, and insert this condition on line 218:

```cpp
if (!sleTrustLine) {
    return tecNO_AUTH;
}
```
